        <div id="pool-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Pool Size and Cable Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every corded robot has a maximum pool length, and the cable has to be long enough to reach the farthest point with slack for the robot to climb walls. Maytronics lists the Nautilus CC Plus and Sigma for pools up to 50 feet with 60-foot cables, and Polaris lists the 9550 Sport for pools up to 60 feet with a 70-foot cable. If your pool is long or L-shaped, measure diagonally from where the power supply will sit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless robots are rated by area rather than length. Retailers list the Aiper Scuba S1 at up to about 1,600 square feet and the Wybot C2 at up to 2,152 square feet, but those figures assume the battery lasts the whole cycle. In practice, a cordless robot that runs short of battery leaves part of the pool uncleaned, so choose one rated comfortably above your pool size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth matters for some robots. Beatbot lists an operating depth of about 0.5 to 3 metres for the AquaSense Pro, so very shallow lounging ledges or very deep diving wells may be outside the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above-ground pools usually need a robot designed for them, because the walls and ladder layout differ. The Dolphin Escape is designed for above-ground pools, but its listed maximum pool size varies between 26 and 33 feet across sources, so check your pool against the manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Floor, Walls and Waterline
          </h2>

          <p>
            Robots differ in what they clean. Entry-level models such as the Dolphin Escape clean the floor only. Mid-range models add walls, and premium models such as the Dolphin Sigma add the waterline, the band at the surface where oils and scum collect. A few, such as the Beatbot AquaSense Pro, also skim the water surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Waterline cleaning is worth paying for in pools with a lot of sunscreen and body oil, because that ring is the hardest part of the pool to keep clean by hand. If your pool is mostly troubled by leaves and dirt on the floor, a floor-and-wall robot is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface skimming is the newest feature and the one with the most mixed reviews. Digital Trends found the AquaSense Pro's skimming works but is slow. If your main problem is floating leaves, a skimmer net or a dedicated skimmer may still be faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Navigation varies too. Higher-end robots use gyroscopes, cameras or programmed patterns to cover the pool systematically rather than bouncing around randomly. That shortens cycles and reduces missed spots, but it is also where real-world performance can fall short of the listing, as one independent review of the Wybot C2 found.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Filters and Suction
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robot's filter decides what it catches. Fine filters catch dust and pollen; coarse filters handle leaves without clogging. Some robots ship with both, and Wybot lists dual 180-micron and 10-micron filtration on the C2. A robot with only a fine filter will clog quickly in a leafy pool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Suction ratings, given in gallons per hour, help compare models from the same brand but are not always comparable across brands. Maytronics lists 4,500 GPH for the Sigma, and Beatbot lists up to 5,500 GPH for the AquaSense Pro from its own lab testing. Treat these as manufacturer figures, not independent measurements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basket and canister size matters for how often you empty the robot. Polaris lists a five-litre canister on the 9550 Sport and retailers list a 3.5-litre basket on the Aiper Scuba S1. Bigger is better in pools with heavy debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the filter after every cycle. A clogged filter cuts suction, makes the motor work harder and can shorten the robot's life, and most warranty problems are easier to avoid than to claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Warranty and Repairs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Warranty is one of the biggest differences between robots. In our research, Maytronics lists three years on the Dolphin Sigma and 2.5 years on the Nautilus CC Plus, Polaris lists two years on the 9550 Sport, and the Dolphin Escape carries two years. A dealer notes that Dolphin warranties are not prorated or limited by cycles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Regional differences are real. A Canadian store listing for a Nautilus CC Plus model shows a one-year warranty, while the main product pages list 2.5 years. Always check the warranty for the exact model and region before buying, and keep the receipt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repairs outside warranty are expensive because the motor, pump and power supply are sealed units. If a robot fails after the warranty ends, the repair can be a large share of the price of a new one, which is why we weight warranty so heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy from a dealer who can handle warranty claims. Robots usually need to be shipped or taken to a service centre, so a local dealer can save weeks without a cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask the dealer what a claim involves before you buy. Some brands repair robots through local service centres, while others ask you to ship the robot or its power supply. Knowing the process in advance saves time if a motor or charger fails in the middle of the swimming season, when every week without a cleaner shows in the water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Care and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Take the robot out of the pool after each cycle unless the maker says otherwise. Leaving it in the water constantly exposes seals and plastics to chlorine and sunlight, and most warranties expect reasonable care.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the power supply in the shade and off the ground, and coil the cable loosely without kinks. A caddy, which comes with the Polaris 9550 Sport and is available for Dolphins, makes moving the robot easier and protects it from being dropped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove the robot when shocking the pool or when chlorine levels are high, and rinse it with fresh water now and then. A <a href="/best-garden-hose/">garden hose</a> and a soft brush are enough to clean the filters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winterise properly where pools close for the season: clean and dry the robot, store it indoors above freezing, and keep cordless batteries partly charged, as most lithium batteries prefer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Cheaper Cleaner Is Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            A robot is not always the best value. If your pool is small, in an open yard with little debris, and your filter pump is already running, a suction-side cleaner may do the job for a fraction of the cost. Our <a href="/best-pool-vacuum/">pool vacuum guide</a> covers those options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have a booster pump, a pressure-side cleaner with its own bag is a strong, cheaper choice for leafy pools. A robot makes most sense when you would otherwise need to add a booster pump, or when you want the pool cleaned without loading your filter at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the deck clean as well. Debris tracked in from the surround ends up in the pool, so a sealed, cleanable deck helps; our guide to <a href="/best-pool-deck-sealer/">pool deck sealers</a> covers the options, and a <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer</a> makes spring cleaning faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to swim longer into the shoulder seasons, a heater matters more than any cleaner; see <a href="/best-pool-heater/">our pool heater picks</a>. And for a smaller body of water that stays hot all year, compare <a href="/best-hot-tub/">our hot tub picks</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

