        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best robotic pool cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most inground pools up to 50 feet, the Dolphin Nautilus CC Plus with Wi-Fi is a strong all-round choice, with a 60-foot cable and a 2.5-year warranty on its main listings. For premium waterline cleaning, the Dolphin Sigma adds a three-year warranty. For longer pools, the Polaris 9550 Sport is rated to 60 feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless robotic pool cleaners good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are convenient and improving quickly, but runtime varies by mode and published figures often conflict. They suit small and mid-size pools well. For large pools cleaned often, a corded robot remains more reliable because it never runs out of charge mid-cycle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robotic pool cleaners need a booster pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Robotic cleaners have their own motor, pump and filter and run from a low-voltage power supply or battery. They do not use your filter pump, skimmer or a booster pump, which is one of their main advantages over pressure-side cleaners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do robotic pool cleaners last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With good care, many owners get several seasons from a robot, but motors and power supplies eventually fail. Warranties in our research run from about two to three years on the main listings. Buy the longest warranty you can and clean the filter after every use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a robotic pool cleaner climb walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most mid-range and premium robots clean walls, and some, such as the Dolphin Sigma, also clean the waterline. Entry-level models such as the Dolphin Escape clean the floor only. Check the coverage listed for the exact model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I run a robotic pool cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many owners run a robot two to three times a week in the swimming season, and more often after storms or heavy use. Robots with schedulers and Wi-Fi apps make this easy. Clean the filter after each run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a robotic pool cleaner worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you would otherwise need a booster pump, or you want the pool cleaned without loading your filter and skimmer, usually yes. For a small, low-debris pool with an existing pump, a suction-side cleaner may be enough and costs far less.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

